From 5861aa216b3ea79ed7b1833c560bbfd0bcd1127b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 04:21:10 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent_tasks_panel: follow a running task's log in a tab of
 the panel
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Zeo next proposal §6.1 and §6.27 (asks 3, 13, 47). A running task's
output could be glanced at -- the card's tail, the eye's preview of a
dozen lines -- or, once the task ended, opened as a saved file in the
editor. Following a long build while it ran meant neither. A running
task with output now has "Follow Log" on its row in the Agent Tasks
panel, which opens its log in a tab of the panel itself.

The list is the first tab, "Tasks"; each log tab carries the task's
label and an X. One tab per task: opening it again focuses it. The tab
reads the log every second while the task runs, scrolls to the end as
lines arrive, and stops following when the wheel turns up to read
earlier lines -- "Follow" resumes. Once the task has ended, one last
read takes its end and the tab says "Task ended" and asks no more.
The text is bounded to 10,000 lines; older ones are dropped and
counted ("N earlier lines not kept"). A line redrawn with carriage
returns shows its last state, as a terminal does.

The reads are incremental. The first asks for the end of the file;
each later one sends `fromByte`, where the last read ended, and gets
back `startByte`, where its content begins (Claude Agent (Plus)
0.37.0). The tab appends a read only when it starts exactly where the
last ended, and starts over otherwise -- a truncated or replaced file,
or an adapter older than `fromByte`, which answers with the end of the
file and no `startByte`; the tab then replaces its text on each read,
slower but never doubled. A read cut at the adapter's cap is followed
at once rather than a second later. No capability bump: an older
adapter ignores the field, an older Zeo ignores the answer's.

Plumbing: TaskOutput gains start_byte; AgentConnection gains
fetch_task_output_from, whose default asks for the end of the file so
every connection keeps working; the ACP connection sends fromByte and
reads startByte (absent: None; present but not an offset: an error
naming it). AcpThread::fetch_task_output_from. TasksCard::held_thread
is public, so the panel reaches the thread a card holds. The card's
own read when a task ends (story 041's saved log) is untouched.

The panel's render is split: the list tab (filters, selection toolbar,
list, preview, pager) moved into render_task_list unchanged, so a log
tab can stand in its place. The new code lives in panel/log_tabs.rs.

Tests: panel::log_tabs::log_buffer_tests (appends a read that
continues; joins a line split across reads; starts over when a read
does not continue; replaces on every read without startByte; keeps
the last state of a carriage-return line; drops the oldest past the
cap and counts them). panel::log_tabs_tests (the button only on a
running task with output; the tab replaces the list, shows the end and
reads on from where it stopped -- reads [end, fromByte 8]; one tab per
task, and closing returns to the list; an ended task is read once more
then not again; a scroll up pauses following and Follow resumes it).
agent_servers: fromByte on the wire and startByte read; no startByte
reads as None; a malformed startByte is refused by name. The fake
connection serves output by offset, as plus does: a queue of answers
was consumed by the card's own end-of-task read. Seen failing with
the button hidden, the offset ignored, the end never stopping reads,
LogBuffer always starting over, the scroll pause a no-op, and the
connection not sending fromByte.
---

